<Read
  slug="the-warning-label"
  description={"What a British law from 1887 can tell Canada about the China panic, and about who has been writing our labels. Every claim footnoted."}
  standfirst={"What a British law from 1887 can tell Canada about the China panic. And about who has been writing our labels."}
>
      <section class="brief" aria-labelledby="in-brief">
        <h2 id="in-brief">In brief</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Dominant powers keep putting warning labels on rising competitors. Britain did it to Germany in 1887. Washington did it to Japan in 1987. It is being done to China now.</li>
          <li>The labels keep failing. &quot;Made in Germany&quot; became a mark of quality. Japan never bought America; its trophy purchase went bankrupt within six years.</li>
          <li>Several of today&#x27;s loudest China claims collapsed on their own record: a spy-chip story nobody could confirm, a balloon the Pentagon says collected nothing, garlic filed as a national security threat, a farmland takeover that measures under one percent.</li>
          <li>Some concerns are real. Crane modems. A connected bus. One riding in Richmond. Real risks need neutral tests, not country labels.</li>
          <li>Canada has been paying for labels written in Washington: canola tariffs, a 100 percent threat over a limited EV deal, and three State Department meetings with Alberta separatists.</li>
          <li>A peer writes its own tests. This paper is an argument for doing that.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <h3>How to read this</h3>
      <p>Every figure in this paper is public, dated and footnoted. Where I give my own opinion, it stands in its own sentence and says so.</p>
      <p>Nothing here asks an American reader to feel insulted. It asks a Canadian reader to feel steady.</p>
      <p class="byline">Jesse James · Northern Temper. Written in View Royal, British Columbia. <a href="#sources">The sources are at the end.</a></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part one</span> The sledgehammer</h2>
      <p>On the first of July, 1987, Helen Delich Bentley, a congresswoman from Maryland, walked onto the lawn of the United States Capitol carrying a sledgehammer. She was there to kill a radio.</p>
      <p>The radio was a Toshiba. Eight colleagues joined her. A day earlier the Senate had voted to ban Toshiba products from the country for two to five years.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></p>
      <p>There was a real offence underneath the theatre. A Toshiba subsidiary had sold precision milling machines to the Soviet Union between 1982 and 1984, against Western export rules, and the company&#x27;s chairman and president resigned over it.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup> Nobody disputes that.</p>
      <p>What the lawn did not show was the rest of the story. A Norwegian police report later found that firms in France, Italy and West Germany had sold the Soviets similar machines.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup> Nobody took a hammer to a Peugeot.</p>
      <p>The footage ran again and again on Japanese television.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup> A Washington Post columnist wrote at the time that the scene &quot;does not give me a lot of confidence in the policy-making skills of our elected legislators.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></p>
      <p class="view"><strong>A confession, in my own voice.</strong> I know the feeling that put the hammer in her hand. Last week I lost my temper in a comment thread about China and told a stranger to go learn something. It felt good for about a minute. It persuaded nobody. That is why I wrote this.</p>
      <p>Two years after the radio, Japan bought America.</p>
      <p>Or so the magazine covers said. In October 1989 Mitsubishi Estate paid $846 million for 51 percent of the Rockefeller Group, the owner of Rockefeller Center.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a>, <a href="#note-4">4</a></sup> Sony had bought Columbia Pictures weeks earlier. The question on the newsstands was whether the United States was for sale.</p>
      <p>Then the Tokyo stock market cratered, weeks after the deal closed.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup> In May 1995 the Rockefeller Center partnerships filed for Chapter 11 and the property passed to its lenders.<sup><a href="#note-4">4</a></sup> Japan went into what its own economists call the lost decades.</p>
      <p>The country that was going to own America ended up owning a bankruptcy.</p>
      <p>The panic had a price that was never counted in dollars. On June 19, 1982, Vincent Chin, a 27-year-old Chinese American draftsman, was beaten with a baseball bat outside a McDonald&#x27;s in Highland Park, Michigan, by two autoworkers who, witnesses said, blamed him for jobs lost to Japanese cars.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a>, <a href="#note-6">6</a></sup> He died four days later, five days before his wedding. Chin was not Japanese. One of his killers pleaded guilty to manslaughter and the other no contest. They received three years&#x27; probation and a $3,000 fine. Neither spent a day in jail.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></p>

      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>1987</dt><dd>One radio. Nine members of Congress. Sledgehammers on the Capitol lawn.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>$846M</dt><dd>Mitsubishi Estate&#x27;s 1989 price for 51 percent of Rockefeller Center&#x27;s owner.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>1995</dt><dd>Rockefeller Center partnerships file for Chapter 11. The trophy goes to the lenders.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>0</dt><dd>Days in jail served by the two men who killed Vincent Chin.</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <p>Hold that last number. We will come back to who pays.</p>
      <p>First, the part of the story that is a full century older than the radio, and that explains it.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part two</span> The label</h2>
      <p>In 1887 the British Parliament passed the Merchandise Marks Act. Sheffield cutlers and other English makers had complained that German firms were copying their goods and passing them off as British. The answer was a law that forced imports to carry their true origin.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup> Three words, meant as a warning to the shopper: Made in Germany.</p>
      <p>Nine years later a journalist named Ernest Edwin Williams turned a run of magazine articles into a book with those three words on the cover. It went through six editions in 1896 alone.<sup><a href="#note-8">8</a></sup> Williams told his readers the phrase was &quot;raw material for a jape at the pantomime&quot; while the nation slept through &quot;the impending danger.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-8">8</a></sup></p>
      <p>The label did its job. It just did the opposite of what it was for.</p>
      <p>Williams himself described the mechanism. A British buyer receives his order stamped Made in Germany, works out that the English middleman took a cut, and decides to &quot;purchase direct from the German houses, and save the commission.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-8">8</a></sup> Imports kept rising. Historians now describe the Act as hidden protectionism that protected nothing and handed German makers free advertising.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup> Within a generation the stamp meant precision, and it still does.<sup><a href="#note-9">9</a></sup></p>
      <p>That is the warning label. A mark meant to scare the buyer that ends up teaching the buyer.</p>
      <p>Every generation of a dominant power writes one. The pattern is older than the panic you are living through, and it has a record. Five entries from that record follow.</p>

      <figure class="timeline" aria-labelledby="fig-1">
        <figcaption id="fig-1" class="fig">Figure 1 · Five warning labels, 139 years</figcaption>
        <ol>
          <li>
