      <p class="fig"><strong>Reading the figure.</strong> Each entry is a dominant economy answering a rising competitor with a label rather than a test. Four of the five have an outcome on record. The fifth is the one this paper is about.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part three</span> The one we wrote on people</h2>
      <p>Canada has written a warning label of its own. We wrote it on people.</p>
      <p>In 1885, as the last spikes went into the Canadian Pacific Railway, Ottawa charged every Chinese newcomer a $50 head tax. It rose to $100 in 1900 and to $500 in 1903, roughly two years&#x27; wages for a labourer at the time.<sup><a href="#note-10">10</a></sup> About 81,000 people paid it, and the federal treasury collected about $23 million.<sup><a href="#note-10">10</a></sup></p>
      <p>In 1923 the Chinese Immigration Act stopped the tax and simply shut the door. Almost no Chinese immigration was allowed until the Act was repealed in 1947.<sup><a href="#note-10">10</a></sup></p>
      <p>On June 22, 2006, Stephen Harper stood in the House of Commons and said: &quot;On behalf of all Canadians and the Government of Canada, we offer a full apology to Chinese Canadians for the head tax and express our deepest sorrow for the subsequent exclusion of Chinese immigrants.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup> He called the measures &quot;race-based financial measures, aimed solely at the Chinese.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></p>
      <p>Symbolic payments of $20,000 went to living payers and to the widows and widowers of payers who had died. By then only about 785 people were left to claim.<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></p>
      <p>I am not comparing a tax on human beings to a tariff on cars. I am saying the reflex is the same. When the competition frightens us, we reach for a label instead of a test.</p>
      <p class="view"><strong>My view:</strong> the 2006 apology only means something if we notice the reflex the next time it shows up.</p>
      <p>It is showing up now. Six examples follow, with what the record shows beside each one.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part four</span> Today&#x27;s labels. The ledger.</h2>
      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Chinese spy chips in American servers</h3>
        <p class="who">Bloomberg Businessweek, &quot;The Big Hack,&quot; Oct. 4, 2018. Seventeen unnamed sources.</p>
        <p><strong>What the record shows.</strong> Apple said it had never found malicious chips, hardware manipulations or planted vulnerabilities in any server. Amazon said that at no time, past or present, had it found modified hardware or malicious chips. US Homeland Security and Britain&#x27;s National Cyber Security Centre said they had no reason to doubt the companies. Supermicro&#x27;s outside investigators found no evidence of malicious hardware. Bloomberg never retracted the story.<sup><a href="#note-12">12</a>, <a href="#note-13">13</a></sup></p>
      </article>
      <article class="entry">
        <h3>A spy balloon stole our secrets</h3>
        <p class="who">February 2023. Senator Marco Rubio: &quot;one more method that they use to collect intelligence on us.&quot;</p>
        <p><strong>What the record shows.</strong> On June 29, 2023, the Pentagon&#x27;s spokesman said the balloon &quot;did not collect while it was transiting the United States.&quot; The Pentagon maintains the balloon had collection capability and belonged to a surveillance programme. By Washington&#x27;s own account, it gathered nothing over American soil.<sup><a href="#note-14">14</a></sup></p>
      </article>
      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Sewage garlic is a national security threat</h3>
        <p class="who">Senator Rick Scott, Dec. 6, 2023, letter to the Commerce Secretary requesting a Section 232 national security investigation of all Chinese garlic.</p>
        <p><strong>What the record shows.</strong> The letter cited practices &quot;well documented&quot; on YouTube and in documentaries. The real grievance underneath is unpaid anti-dumping duties on garlic, which is a trade matter with a trade remedy. A clove of garlic has never been a security matter.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></p>
      </article>
      <article class="entry">
        <h3>China is buying up the farmland</h3>
        <p class="who">A recurring claim in US state and federal legislatures, 2021 to 2025.</p>
        <p><strong>What the record shows.</strong> US Department of Agriculture data for the end of 2023 puts Chinese investors at about 0.6 percent of foreign-held American farmland, which is about 0.02 percent of all privately held farmland. The largest foreign holder, with a third of all foreign-held acres, is Canada.<sup><a href="#note-16">16</a>, <a href="#note-17">17</a></sup> Figure 2 shows the proportions.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Chinese police stations in Montreal</h3>
        <p class="who">RCMP, March 2023, publicly naming two community groups on the South Shore and in Montreal.</p>
        <p><strong>What the record shows.</strong> In September 2025 the RCMP confirmed it had closed the investigation. No charges were laid. The two groups have sued the force for defamation.<sup><a href="#note-18">18</a>, <a href="#note-19">19</a></sup> Two community centres carried the label for two and a half years.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Traitors in Parliament</h3>
        <p class="who">June 2024. A parliamentary intelligence committee report describes some MPs as &quot;witting&quot; participants in foreign interference.</p>
        <p><strong>What the record shows.</strong> Justice Marie-Josée Hogue&#x27;s final report, Jan. 28, 2025: &quot;I did not see evidence of &#x27;traitors&#x27; in Parliament.&quot; She found the committee&#x27;s conclusions &quot;more definitive than the underlying intelligence could support.&quot; In the same report she named the People&#x27;s Republic of China as the main perpetrator of foreign interference against Canada.<sup><a href="#note-20">20</a>, <a href="#note-21">21</a></sup> Both findings are true at once.</p>
      </article>
      <p class="fig"><strong>On method.</strong> Each entry pairs the claim as it was made with the finding of the body best placed to check it: the companies named, the Pentagon, the USDA, the RCMP, a public inquiry. Where a claim has a true part, the entry says so.</p>

      <BarTable name="Figure 2" rowHead="Holder" columns={['Share of foreign-held US farmland, end of 2023']} unit="percent" rows={[
        { label: 'Canada', values: [33.5] },
        { label: 'All other countries', values: [65.9] },
        { label: 'China', values: [0.6] },
      ]}>Figure 2 · Who holds America&#x27;s foreign-held farmland. Source: USDA Farm Service Agency, AFIDA annual report through Dec. 31, 2023.<sup><a href="#note-16">16</a>, <a href="#note-17">17</a></sup></BarTable>
      <p class="fig">Foreign holders had about 45 million acres at the end of 2023, 3.5 percent of private US farmland. Canada held about 15.35 million acres, the largest foreign holder by a wide margin. China held about 277,000 acres, roughly 0.02 percent of all private US farmland. All other countries combined include the Netherlands, Italy, the United Kingdom, Germany and Portugal.</p>
      <p class="fig"><strong>Reading the figure.</strong> If foreign ownership of farmland is a security question, the data says the question is mostly about Canadians. Nobody in Congress has proposed a hearing on that, which tells you the concern was never about acres.</p>
      <p class="fig">The narrow version of the worry is real: specific parcels next to military bases deserve review, whoever buys them. That is a test. &quot;China is buying the farmland&quot; is a label.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part five</span> The ones that are real</h2>
      <p>I would be lying to you if I said every warning was false. Three are not.</p>
      <p><strong>Cranes.</strong> A joint congressional investigation found cellular modems on Chinese-made port cranes that were &quot;not requested by U.S. ports or included in contracts.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-22">22</a></sup> Port staff believed they were for maintenance. No theft was shown. It is still a back door nobody ordered.</p>
      <p><strong>Buses.</strong> Norway&#x27;s transit operator Ruter tested a Chinese-built Yutong bus in a secure facility and found the maker had remote access to the battery and power supply over the mobile network.<sup><a href="#note-23">23</a></sup> Nobody could steer it from the factory. It could, in theory, be stopped. Ruter&#x27;s comparison was an older European bus with little connectivity, which, as one later analysis put it, could not establish that the risk was uniquely Chinese.<sup><a href="#note-24">24</a></sup></p>
      <p><strong>Richmond.</strong> Justice Hogue found a &quot;reasonable possibility&quot; that false claims spread on WeChat affected the 2021 result in Steveston-Richmond East, where Kenny Chiu lost his seat. Strong indicators of PRC involvement. No definitive link.<sup><a href="#note-25">25</a></sup></p>
      <p>Here is the thing about all three. None of them is solved by a label.</p>
      <p>The fix for a connected bus from Zhengzhou is the fix for a connected bus from Munich: no remote access to safety systems, whoever built it. The fix for a crane modem is a contract that lists what is in the crane. The fix for disinformation on WeChat is the one we still need for Facebook.</p>
      <p>A test applies to everyone and tells you something about the next bus. A label applies to one country and tells you nothing.</p>
      <p class="view"><strong>My view:</strong> Canada should be harder on remote access to critical systems than it is today, and it should be hard on every vendor, including our own.</p>
      <p>Which brings us to the bill. Because somebody has been paying for labels, and it has not been the people writing them.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part six</span> Who pays for our labels</h2>
      <p>In 2024 Canada put a 100 percent tariff on Chinese electric vehicles and a 25 percent surtax on Chinese steel and aluminum. The numbers matched Washington&#x27;s.<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a></sup> China answered in March 2025 with 100 percent tariffs on Canadian canola oil, canola meal and peas, and 25 percent on pork and seafood, then added duties on canola seed in August.<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a></sup> Prairie farmers paid for a label written somewhere else.</p>
      <p>On January 16, 2026, Mark Carney came home from Beijing with a limited deal: up to 49,000 Chinese EVs a year at a 6.1 percent tariff, and China&#x27;s combined canola-seed duty falling from 84 percent to about 15 percent.<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a>, <a href="#note-27">27</a></sup> Carney said Canada had &quot;no intention&quot; of a free-trade deal with China or any other non-market economy.<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a></sup></p>
      <p>That same day Donald Trump said that if Canada could get a deal with China, it should do that.<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a></sup></p>
      <p>Eight days later he posted that if Canada made a deal with China it would &quot;immediately be hit with a 100% Tariff against all Canadian goods,&quot; and that &quot;China will eat Canada alive.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a>, <a href="#note-29">29</a></sup></p>
      <p>The month had one more entry. The Financial Times reported that US State Department officials had met three times since the previous April with the Alberta Prosperity Project, a group collecting signatures for a referendum on leaving Canada.<sup><a href="#note-30">30</a>, <a href="#note-31">31</a></sup> The group&#x27;s counsel said it would return to Washington to discuss a $500 billion credit facility for an independent Alberta.<sup><a href="#note-31">31</a></sup> Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent called Alberta &quot;a natural partner for the U.S.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-30">30</a></sup> A White House official said administration representatives meet with many civil society groups and that no commitments were made.<sup><a href="#note-32">32</a></sup></p>
      <p>Americans are entitled to write their own labels. That is their business. What is our business is that we keep paying for them.</p>

      <figure class="timeline dated" aria-labelledby="fig-3">
        <figcaption id="fig-3" class="fig">Figure 3 · January 2026, in two weeks</figcaption>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p class="yr"><time datetime="2026-01-16">Jan 16</time></p>
            <p><strong>Beijing.</strong> Carney and Xi agree a limited tariff-quota deal: 49,000 EVs a year at 6.1 percent, canola-seed duty cut from 84 to about 15 percent.<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a>, <a href="#note-27">27</a></sup></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="yr"><time datetime="2026-01-16">Jan 16</time></p>
            <p><strong>Washington.</strong> Asked about it, Trump says that if Canada can get a deal with China, it should.<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a></sup></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="yr"><time datetime="2026-01-24">Jan 24</time></p>
            <p><strong>Truth Social.</strong> &quot;If Canada makes a deal with China, it will immediately be hit with a 100% Tariff against all Canadian goods.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a>, <a href="#note-29">29</a></sup></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="yr"><time datetime="2026-01-29">Jan 29</time></p>
            <p><strong>Ottawa.</strong> After the FT reports three State Department meetings with Alberta separatists, Carney says he expects Washington to &quot;respect Canadian sovereignty.&quot; BC Premier David Eby calls the meetings &quot;treason.&quot;<sup><a href="#note-30">30</a>, <a href="#note-32">32</a></sup></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </figure>

      <figure class="stat">
        <p class="big">1,019 days</p>
        <figcaption>Michael Kovrig and Michael Spavor were detained in China in December 2018, nine days after Canada arrested Huawei&#x27;s Meng Wanzhou on a United States extradition request. They flew home on September 24, 2021, the evening Washington settled its case with Meng by agreement and the extradition was dropped.<sup><a href="#note-33">33</a>, <a href="#note-34">34</a></sup> Beijing took the hostages. That is on Beijing. The warrant was written in New York. The time was served by two Canadians.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="fig"><strong>Reading the figure.</strong> Eight days separate &quot;you should do that&quot; from the 100 percent threat. Nothing in the deal changed in between. What changed was Carney&#x27;s Davos speech against economic coercion. The lesson is not about one president. It is that a label written for someone else&#x27;s politics can land on a Saskatchewan canola field with no warning at all.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Part seven</span> What a peer does</h2>
      <p class="view"><strong>What follows is my view,</strong> stated as plainly as I can.</p>
      <p>A country that has read its own history writes tests, not labels. Remote access to a safety system is banned, whoever built it. A crane&#x27;s contract lists what is in the crane. A port, a grid, a network gets a standard, and the standard does not check passports.</p>
      <p>It keeps its own farmers out of other people&#x27;s trade wars. When a tariff is copied from a neighbour, somebody should be able to say which Canadian interest it serves. In 2024 nobody did, and the canola bill arrived the following March.</p>
      <p>It keeps its neighbours&#x27; respect. Nothing in this paper asks an American to feel small. The United States is our largest customer and our oldest friend, and friends can disagree about China without one of them paying for the other&#x27;s argument.</p>
      <p>It keeps Chinese Canadians out of the crossfire. Justice Hogue found that Beijing is the main foreign interferer in this country, and the people most exposed to that interference are Chinese Canadians. They are also the people most exposed when a police force names a community centre and then lays no charges. The 2006 apology was for a label. The next one should not be necessary.</p>
      <figure class="mark">
        <BearDual />
      </figure>
      <p>And it keeps its temper. The two bears above are not roaring at anyone. They stand back to back, looking outward, watching. That is the whole idea.</p>
      <p class="pull">The warning label has a 139-year record. It fails the country that writes it. It teaches the buyer. And it costs people who had nothing to do with the argument.</p>
      <p>We can read that record, or we can be the next entry in it. I know which I would rather be. The choice, as it should be, is yours.</p>

